From: PS5_Proton <local>
Subject: [PATCH] gpu-broker: say where each GPU client's frame time goes

A Direct3D 12 game plays at 28 to 34 fps through the broker (2026-10-17, record removed from the tree; in git history: frame p50 29.5 to 36 ms,
present_avg 5.8 ms), and nothing measured how long the GPU worked on a frame, so it could not be said whether the GPU,
the presents or the game's and Wine's CPU set the frame time. Beside each client's gpu-pacing line, a gpu-split line
over the same window (src/pw_gpu_timing.h) now gives, per shown frame:
- with PROTON_GPU_TIMESTAMPS=1 (read at start; off, no submission changes): the GPU's busy time. Each submission is
  wrapped in the two timestamps RADV writes for vkCmdWriteTimestamp, a top-of-pipe COPY_DATA of the GPU clock and a
  bottom-of-pipe RELEASE_MEM, into its ring slot's 16 bytes of a page made beside the fence page; when the fence passes
  it, the part of [start, end] after the previous submission's end goes to its client. The clock's rate is measured
  against CLOCK_MONOTONIC across the run.
- always: the submissions, the broker's whole present and its copy of the frame into the display's buffer, the
  broker's handling of the submissions, and the client's own share, which it sends about once a second
  (PW_GPU_TIMING): its wait for the frame's fence before presenting (the WSI), its cache flush of the frame, the
  present's round trip, and its submissions' round trips.
---
--- a/tooling/gpu-broker/client.c
+++ b/tooling/gpu-broker/client.c
@@ -218,13 +218,42 @@
                          .argument={width,height,pitch,cycle}};
